### What each test proves

| Test | Requirement proven |
|---|---|
| `TestCycleAbortLowestPriority` | A wait-for cycle is detected; the **lowest-priority** member is aborted; ties break on larger ID deterministically; the victim's partial writes are rolled back (including a key the survivor never touches), and the survivor commits. |
| `TestUpgradeRetainsPosition` | A read→write upgrade keeps its original admission `Position` and is granted ahead of a later higher-priority writer; two upgrading readers deadlock and the earlier-arrived one wins; read-modify-write upgrades are stored as **resolved absolute values** in the token. |
| `TestReplayByteIdenticalAcrossReplicas` | Two independently replayed replicas are byte-identical to each other and to the primary, and match the digest embedded in the token — even with read-dependent writes and an aborted transaction. |
| `TestTokenIsDeterministic` | Running the same batch twice produces identical `Order`, `Aborted`, `Commits`, and digest (no map-iteration or scheduling nondeterminism). |
| `TestIdempotentReplayAfterPartialFailure` | For every crash point `k`, resuming with `ReplayFrom(token, partialState, k)` converges on the full replay, and re-applying the whole token is a no-op — idempotent replay. |
| `TestReplayFromRejectsBadOffset` | Bounds/`nil` handling for the replay API. |

The solution is self-contained: create the four files above in a Go 1.26 module and run the commands. No external dependencies.
